---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { breadcrumbLd, truncate } from "@/lib/seo";

const path = "/privacy";
const title = "Privacy · localmodel.run";
const description = truncate(
  "How localmodel.run handles data: no account, no tracking cookies, cookieless analytics, and hardware choices computed in your browser. We collect as little as possible.",
);

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbLd([{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Privacy", url: path }], Astro.site!.href),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-10">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Privacy", url: path }]} />

    <div class="mt-4 mb-6">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>localmodel.run &middot; data policy</p>
      <h1 class="text-balance text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Privacy</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
        <span class="panel-label">policy overview</span>
        <span class="panel-sub">static site &middot; aggregate analytics</span>
      </div>

      <div class="px-5 py-5 space-y-6 text-muted-foreground">
        <p>
          localmodel.run is a static site. There is no application backend, no account, and no sign-up. It was built to collect as little as possible, and this page says exactly what that means in plain terms.
        </p>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">What we don't do</p>
          <p class="text-foreground font-semibold mb-2">No accounts or visitor profiles.</p>
          <p>
            No accounts, no logins, no email capture. No advertising, no third-party trackers, no tracking cookies. We do not sell visitor data.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">Analytics</p>
          <p class="text-foreground font-semibold mb-2">Cookieless aggregate counts only.</p>
          <p>
            To see which pages are useful and where visitors come from, we use <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a>. It is cookieless, does not fingerprint you, and reports only aggregate counts. It cannot identify you.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3">
            Because each model and device combination has its own page, visiting one is counted as an aggregate page view. That tells us a page was popular, not who looked at it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3">
            The guided setup offers voluntary “It worked” and “I got stuck” links. These counts are self-reported feedback, not verified successful installations. Your choices stay in the URL fragment after the # so you can return to the same setup. Fragments are not sent in page requests or referrers.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3">
            Optional action counts use Cloudflare Zaraz with no added analytics SDK, cookies, or visitor IDs. Each request contains only a fixed action name, such as a fit check, setup link, successful command copy, runtime download link, browser demo load or completion, or setup feedback. No model, hardware, URL, prompt, image, audio, or output is attached. These are action totals, not unique people or a verified conversion rate. Action counts respect Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3">
            We also use Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools. Those report aggregate search statistics from Google and Bing. They run on the search engine's side and are not scripts loaded on this page.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">Your hardware</p>
          <p class="text-foreground font-semibold mb-2">Stays on your device.</p>
          <p>
            When you use the compatibility detector, the model and device you pick are used to compute the answer in your browser. The detector does not send these choices to us or save them in browser storage. Guided setup links keep the choices in the URL fragment, which can remain in your browser history until you clear it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">Local storage</p>
          <p class="text-foreground font-semibold mb-2">Theme preference and a testing opt-out.</p>
          <p>
            Your light or dark theme choice is saved in your browser's local storage so the site remembers it next time. If you use the qa=1 testing flag, a boolean in session storage disables action counting for that tab until you close it or use qa=0. Neither preference identifies you or leaves your browser.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">Hosting</p>
          <p class="text-foreground font-semibold mb-2">Cloudflare Pages.</p>
          <p>
            The site is served by Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes standard request data such as your IP address to deliver and protect the site. See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a> for how they handle it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="wire"></div>

        <section>
          <p class="stat-label mb-2">Questions</p>
          <p>
            Questions about privacy? Find me on <a href="https://x.com/AnsumanShah" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline">X</a> or <a href="https://github.com/ansumanshah" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline">GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="panel-foot">
        <span class="panel-sub">last updated 1 november 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
